{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790966784,"nonce":"bNUk5x/26WSqO2UEumfyUr8690Y=","title":"hash_composite_scratch must hash layer_commands or CSS-animated layer-opacity freezes the","message":"`hash_composite_scratch` in `crates/blinc_app/src/context.rs` decides whether to re-bake a CSS-composited subtree's per-node `LayerTexture` each frame. Originally it hashed only `primitives`, `foreground_primitives`, and `aux_data`. **It did NOT hash `layer_commands`.**\n\nThat worked for the simple case: an opacity-animated leaf whose own `RenderProps.opacity` is captured in its primitives. It fails for composite-promoted SUBTREES whose root pushes an animating `layer_commands` `push_layer { opacity: t }` around the whole subtree. The descendant primitives are byte-identical every frame (cn::input's bg, border, text), so the primitives hash matches, the dirty-skip succeeds, and the texture stays frozen at the **frame-1 sample** of the animation — which is `t ≈ 0` for a fade-in.\n\nSymptom that surfaced this: cn::input / cn::textarea opened inside a cn::popover with a `cn-popover-enter` CSS animation (animates opacity + 2D transform → `is_composite_promotable()`). The walker pushed a composite layer for the entire popover root subtree on every paint. The very first paint baked the texture at near-zero layer opacity. Subsequent paints had identical primitives → no re-bake → invisible cn::input until mouse-move tripped the staleness sweep (`composite_css_layers_overlay`'s release-on-demotion path) which released the texture, the slow walker emitted into the regular batch, and the popup finally rendered correctly.\n\n**Fix:** `hash_composite_scratch` must include `layer_commands`. `LayerCommand` is a non-Pod enum, so manually hash the discriminant + the fields that animate per frame:\n- `Push { config }` → opacity bits, blend-mode discriminant, depth\n- `Pop` → discriminant only (constant payload)\n- `Sample { source, dest, .. }` → both rect's x/y/w/h bits\n\nAlso hash `primitive_index` / `foreground_primitive_index` from each `LayerCommandEntry` so a layer's primitive-range shift counts.\n\n**Companion fix:** `invalidate_render_cache_tagged` originally only touched the SDF batches + cached_glyphs / cached_svgs / static_layer. It bypassed `css_composited_textures`, `css_composited_scratch_hash`, `motion_subtree_textures`, and `motion_subtree_scratch_hash`. Any caller that invalidates because the upstream tree state changed (subtree rebuild applied, post-focus state change) needs to also release these per-node textures so the next bake captures the new state instead of reusing a stale texture under an unchanged primitive hash. Drain via `layer_texture_cache_mut().release(tex)` to return the wgpu allocation to the pool.\n\n**Cleaner architectural fix (follow-up):** `apply_all_css_animation_props` shouldn't write the composite-promotable property (opacity / 2D transform) into `render_node.props` for subtrees the walker will route through `push_composite_layer`. The bake should capture REST state; `composite_css_layers_overlay` is solely responsible for animating the per-frame blit. The current shape works only because the bake reads near-zero opacity → texture is near-blank → blit at climbing opacity → result is still near-blank for several frames until the keyframe interpolates upward. Mirrors the P4.3 motion-subtree `bake_skip` already at `paint/motion.rs:750-758` for the motion case.\n\nDetection: if a CSS-animated promoted subtree paints \"stuck at the first frame\" of its animation, the layer-command hash is the prime suspect. Symptom is the texture path; check for an active `push_composite_layer` around the visible content.","files":null}]}